<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort William, Kolkata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1700; rebuilt <a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort William is a fortress on the east bank of the Hooghly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, named after King William III. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> built the first fort around 1700 as the seat of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Presidency; after the Battle of Plassey a much larger fort was built nearby, completed in 1781. It gave its name to the presidency and today houses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a>'s Eastern Command.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-william-kolkata.jpg" alt="Fort William, Kolkata" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Wood (fl. 1827–1833) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fortwilliam1828.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
